1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Weaver — the datacenter without walls.
Open-weight models, routed by measured speed, paid per job.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Every forge signs the output it serves.
No proof, no payment — the escrow enforces it.

3
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Sub-second settlement on Monad.
Portable reputation for every forge — ERC-8004 on-chain.

4
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,900
This is Weaver live: any GPU joins permissionlessly and
competes on measured latency — every inference settles
on Monad testnet with a cryptographic delivery proof.

5
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:48,000
I submit a prompt. No server picking: the scheduler measures
ETR — network RTT, GPU queue, decode speed — and routes to
the fastest warm forge.

6
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:02,900
The pipeline streams live: FIRE accepts, ROUTE dispatches on
measured ETR, EXECUTE streams the tokens, SETTLE primes the
claim. Everything logged: worker, TTFT, delivery hash.

7
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Now we break it on purpose: kill the primary forge
mid-traffic. Residential nodes drop — the network must not.

8
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:25,000
The gateway catches the dead socket instantly, fails over to
the standby forge — the stream completes without a single
error. Real process kill, not a simulation.

9
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:35,700
And this is not a simulation: the failover job settled
on-chain. The forge signed the result hash with its secp256k1
key — WeaverEscrow verified via ecrecover before paying.

10
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:59,700
This receipt is read live from the public Monad RPC:
Released job, 0.01 USDC transferred to the failover forge,
proof hash bound to the served output. Verifiable by anyone.

11
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:07,800
Finally, Zero Data Retention: prompts live in forge RAM and
die with the socket. The chain only sees attestations and
settlement. Weaver — no walls, no tolls, no spying.
